-- Passage rg4-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4ff24455-ae33-51cc-8447-f1d61490bf07', 'rg4-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Day the Sun Went Dark$q$, $q$Account 1: A Journal Entry by Mila Okafor, Age 10

April 8. Today our whole class went out to the soccer field to watch the eclipse. We wore special paper glasses so we would not hurt our eyes. At first the sun looked like a cookie with one small bite missing. Then the bite got bigger and bigger. The air turned cool, and I wished I had brought my jacket. When the sun was almost covered, the birds in the trees stopped singing. My friend Dev grabbed my arm and whispered, "This is so weird!" I felt a little shiver, but it was a happy shiver. I will never forget it.

Account 2: A Report in the Town Newsletter, One Week Later

Last Monday, people across Maple Valley watched a partial solar eclipse. An eclipse happens when the moon passes between Earth and the sun and blocks some of its light. In our town, the moon covered about nine tenths of the sun. The event lasted a little over two hours from start to finish. Workers at the county science center measured a drop in temperature of about eight degrees. More than four hundred students from three schools watched safely with eclipse glasses. The next eclipse that can be seen from Maple Valley will not happen for many years.$q$, 'draft')
