-- Passage rg4-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('100dc7b8-ce9d-53d3-87bc-98a28773aa14', 'rg4-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Footprints in the Riverbed$q$, $q$Account 1: A Hiking Journal by Marco Silva

June 21. The river is very low this summer because we have had almost no rain. My sister Lucia and I walked along the dry riverbed looking for smooth rocks. Then Lucia stopped and pointed at the ground. There was a print in the stone as big as a dinner plate, with three thick toes! We found five more in a row. I put my hand inside one, and it felt like shaking hands with something from long ago. We took photos and sent them to the state park office before we hiked home.

Account 2: An Article in a Science Magazine for Kids

This summer, a dry spell uncovered a trail of dinosaur footprints in a riverbed at Cedar Bend State Park. Two young hikers spotted the tracks and sent photos to park staff. Scientists later visited the site and counted 23 footprints. They believe the tracks were made about 110 million years ago by a plant-eating dinosaur that walked on two legs. Mud slowly hardened into stone, which saved the prints. When the rain returns, the river will cover the tracks again. Scientists made careful molds so the footprints can be studied later.$q$, 'draft')
