-- Passage rg4-character-setting-plot-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1e370e2f-a81f-5475-b08e-6420c8cfa664', 'rg4-character-setting-plot-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Ice Wagon$q$, $q$In the summer of 1925, nine-year-old Elias Moreno helped his uncle deliver ice. There were no electric refrigerators on Pine Street yet. Each family kept food cold in a wooden icebox, and the ice melted a little every day.

Every morning, Uncle Rafael hitched Pepper, their gray horse, to the wagon. The wagon was packed with big blocks of ice under heavy canvas. The canvas kept the sun off so the blocks would not melt too fast.

Elias had one job. He watched for the cards in the windows. Each card had a number printed on each side: 25, 50, 75 and 100. A family turned the card so the number at the top showed how many pounds of ice they wanted.

On the hottest day of July, Elias saw a problem. The card in the Novak family's window had fallen down. It lay flat on the windowsill with no number showing. "Should we skip them today?" Uncle Rafael asked.

Elias thought about it. Mrs. Novak had a new baby and always looked tired. He remembered that she always bought 50 pounds. "I think she just forgot," he said. "Without ice, her milk will spoil in this heat."

He knocked on the door. Mrs. Novak answered, holding the baby. "Oh, thank you!" she said. "I put the card up in a hurry and never checked it."

That evening, Uncle Rafael let Elias hold Pepper's reins for the whole ride home.$q$, 'draft')
