-- Passage rg4-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2d89fba3-f9c7-530a-85a4-b2eeb3ce2e97', 'rg4-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Traffic Light Knows When to Change$q$, $q$Have you ever waited at a red light when no cars were coming the other way? Long ago, most traffic lights ran on a timer. They changed every minute or so, even if the streets were empty. Today, many traffic lights can tell when cars are waiting.

One common tool is a loop of wire buried under the road. You can sometimes see its outline as a thin line cut into the pavement near the stop line. Electricity runs through the wire all the time. When a car stops on top of the loop, the large metal body of the car changes how the electricity flows. A small computer in a box beside the road notices the change. It then knows a car is waiting.

Other lights use cameras mounted above the street. The camera sends pictures to the computer, which looks for cars in each lane.

Once the computer knows where cars are waiting, it decides how long each light should stay green. If a long line of cars is waiting on one street, that street may get a longer green light. If no one is waiting, the light may stay red on that side.

These smart signals help traffic move more smoothly. Drivers spend less time waiting, and cars burn less fuel while sitting still.$q$, 'draft')
